<body>
<div class="deck">
  <div class="decor" id="decor"></div>
  <div class="progress"><div class="progress-fill" id="progressFill"></div></div>
  <div class="chrome">
    <div>← → / space to move</div>
    <div class="page mono"><b id="pageNum">01</b> / <span id="pageTotal">15</span></div>
  </div>

  <main id="slides">

    <!-- 1 -->
    <section class="slide">
      <div class="col">
        <p class="kicker">PLS 210 · Research Design Part 1 · Field Notebook</p>
        <h1 class="big">Family Expectations, Political Voice</h1>
        <p class="lede">How young women in Kazakhstan describe the role of family expectations in shaping their sense of ability to participate in or influence politics. An interpretivist study, Fall 2026.</p>
        <p class="byline">by mina (pseudonym) · the interpretivist tradition</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 2 -->
    <section class="slide">
      <div class="col">
        <p class="kicker">The puzzle</p>
        <h2>The old measure of ability</h2>
        <ul class="notes">
          <li>Political efficacy is usually measured on a scale: do you feel able to influence government, yes or no.</li>
          <li>For a young woman weighing what her family expects of her, that "ability" may not start at the ballot box.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="tagline">It may start at the dinner table — in what she is allowed to say out loud, and to whom.</p>
        <span class="cite-chip">Ahn, 2025</span>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 3 -->
    <section class="slide">
      <div class="col wide">
        <p class="kicker">My research question</p>
        <h1 class="big">How do young women in Kazakhstan describe the role of family expectations in shaping their sense of ability to participate in or influence politics?</h1>
        <span class="cite-chip">Fall 2026</span>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 4: epistemology, expanded -->
    <section class="slide">
      <div class="col wide">
        <p class="kicker">Why not just run a survey · epistemology</p>
        <h2>Why interpretivism, specifically</h2>
        <div class="compare">
          <div class="box muted">
            <h3>A SURVEY WOULD ASK</h3>
            <p>"Do you feel able to influence government?" — circle one, 1 to 4.</p>
            <p class="small">Decides in advance what "ability" means, before she gets a chance to answer.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="box highlight">
            <h3>I'M ASKING INSTEAD</h3>
            <p class="small" style="margin-bottom:.5rem;">A question I'd actually ask a respondent — not myself:</p>
            <p>"What do you expect of yourself, as a woman — and how much of that comes from your family? Has that shaped whether you feel you could ever have a say in something political?"</p>
            <p class="small">The expectation has to be <em>hers</em> now, even if it started with her family — not a rule she's simply following.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="why-row">
          <div class="mini"><b>Efficacy is felt before it's measured</b>A scale can't tell me what a daughter privately concludes about her own voice.</div>
          <div class="mini"><b>Expectation lives in meaning</b>A disapproving silence at dinner is data no survey question captures.</div>
          <div class="mini"><b>I'm asking her to narrate a negotiation</b>A patriarchal bargain is lived and described, not simply observed.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 5: positioning map -->
    <section class="slide">
      <div class="col wide">
        <p class="kicker">What this adds · a map, not a bullet list</p>
        <h2>Where existing work has already looked</h2>
        <div class="map-wrap">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 620 430" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
            <line x1="90" y1="40" x2="90" y2="380" stroke="var(--rule)" stroke-width="2"/>
            <line x1="90" y1="380" x2="580" y2="380" stroke="var(--rule)" stroke-width="2"/>
            <text x="70" y="55" text-anchor="end" font-size="11" fill="var(--ink-faint)">own words</text>
            <text x="70" y="368" text-anchor="end" font-size="11" fill="var(--ink-faint)">aggregate data</text>
            <text x="110" y="404" font-size="11" fill="var(--ink-faint)">not yet in politics</text>
            <text x="470" y="404" font-size="11" fill="var(--ink-faint)">already inside politics</text>

            <circle cx="180" cy="345" r="9" fill="var(--card)" stroke="var(--ink-faint)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <text x="196" y="341" font-size="12" fill="var(--ink)" font-weight="700">Ahn 2025 · Lipovka et al. 2023</text>
            <text x="196" y="356" font-size="11" fill="var(--ink-faint)">large surveys, general population</text>

            <circle cx="200" cy="255" r="9" fill="var(--card)" stroke="var(--ink-faint)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <text x="216" y="251" font-size="12" fill="var(--ink)" font-weight="700">Nurpeissova et al. 2026</text>
            <text x="216" y="266" font-size="11" fill="var(--ink-faint)">survey + focus groups, youth</text>

            <circle cx="430" cy="150" r="9" fill="var(--card)" stroke="var(--ink-faint)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <text x="446" y="146" font-size="12" fill="var(--ink)" font-weight="700">Tourtellotte 2023</text>
            <text x="446" y="161" font-size="11" fill="var(--ink-faint)">ethnography, mobilizing mothers</text>

            <circle cx="500" cy="75" r="9" fill="var(--card)" stroke="var(--ink-faint)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <text x="516" y="71" font-size="12" fill="var(--ink)" font-weight="700">Kakabayeva 2021</text>
            <text x="516" y="86" font-size="11" fill="var(--ink-faint)">interviews, activists &amp; MPs</text>

            <circle cx="150" cy="85" r="14" fill="var(--cherry)"/>
            <text x="150" y="58" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="var(--cherry)" font-weight="700">this study</text>
            <text x="150" y="114" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="var(--ink-soft)">interviews, not-yet-decided</text>
          </svg>
        </div>
        <p class="tagline">Every study that asks women directly asks women already inside politics. Nobody has asked the much larger group still deciding whether family gives them permission to have a voice at all.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 6: theory & frameworks -->
    <section class="slide">
      <div class="col wide">
        <p class="kicker">Theory &amp; frameworks · what I'm building on</p>
        <h2>Four frameworks doing different jobs</h2>
        <div class="card-grid">
          <div class="card">
            <h3>Patriarchal Bargain</h3>
            <p>Women negotiate inside constraints rather than simply obey or resist them.</p>
            <p class="so-what"><span>What this gives me:</span> a way to read compliance as strategy, not just submission.</p>
            <span class="cite-chip">Kandiyoti, 1988</span>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <h3>Role Congruity Theory</h3>
            <p>People project family gender roles onto political leadership itself.</p>
            <p class="so-what"><span>What this gives me:</span> explains why family power distribution predicts political stereotypes.</p>
            <span class="cite-chip">Eagly &amp; Karau, 2002</span>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <h3>Family Political Socialization</h3>
            <p>Family is where political identity first forms — authoritarian structures transmit it more forcefully, and unevenly by gender.</p>
            <p class="so-what"><span>What this gives me:</span> the mechanism connecting "family" to "political self."</p>
            <span class="cite-chip">Van Ditmars, 2023</span>
            <span class="cite-chip">Türköz, 2025</span>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <h3>Interpretive Research Design</h3>
            <p>Meaning-making, not measurement, is the proper object of study for a question like mine.</p>
            <p class="so-what"><span>What this gives me:</span> the methodological foundation for the whole project.</p>
            <span class="cite-chip">Schwartz-Shea &amp; Yanow, 2012</span>
          </div>
