        <p class="kicker">What others have found · zooming in</p>
        <h2>Three groups, one gap</h2>
        <div class="funnel">
          <div class="panel">
            <h3>The mechanism, region-wide</h3>
            <p>Patriarchal norms, not religion, predict lower internal political efficacy — and family is where that gets taught.</p>
            <p class="so-what">Means for my study: gives me the claim to test against her own words, and warns me "ability" is taught early, inside the family.</p>
            <div class="cite-list">
              <span class="cite-chip">Ahn, 2025</span><span class="cite-chip">Van Ditmars, 2023</span><span class="cite-chip">POMEPS, 2020</span><span class="cite-chip">Türköz, 2025</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="arrow">→</div>
          <div class="panel">
            <h3>The discourse reaches here</h3>
            <p>"Traditional values" politics extends into Central Asia and Kazakhstan, and patriarchal family power already correlates with political attitudes there.</p>
            <p class="so-what">Means for my study: not an imported question — the discourse and the correlation are already active where my respondents live.</p>
            <div class="cite-list">
